('35be0d67-dd39-5de6-acda-a45a5c82d5be'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In △QRS, RT is an altitude.\n[Figure: Triangle QRS with R at top, T on QS; RT ⊥ QS; QT = 3 cm; SR = 5 cm.]\nWhich additional condition would not be sufficient to prove that QR = SR?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"T is the midpoint of QS."},{"key":"B","text":"RT bisects ∠QRS."},{"key":"C","text":"TS = 2 cm"},{"key":"D","text":"RT = 4 cm"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-31","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-31.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-31","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-31)."'::jsonb),
('0d2b9a4a-44e5-5823-a5c5-15771653b901'::uuid,'est2','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Lines r, t, n, and v intersect as shown to form isosceles trapezoid ABCD.\n[Figure: Lines n and v are parallel (n above v); lines r and t are the legs, crossing above n and forming angle 1 at their intersection; B and C are on n, A and D on v; the angle at D below line v to the right of t measures (7y − 4)°.]\nWhich expression represents the measure of ∠1 in degrees?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"180 ÷ (7y − 4)"},{"key":"G","text":"180 − 2(7y − 4)"},{"key":"H","text":"(7y − 4)"},{"key":"J","text":"180 − (7y − 4)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-32","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-32.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-32","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"G"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-32)."'::jsonb),
('b9a582b8-b141-5072-9e42-071c636f727f'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','numeric','"A banner is composed of two congruent triangles and a rectangle, as shown below.\n[Figure: A 17 cm by 10 cm rectangle with a congruent triangle attached to each end; each triangle has base 10 cm and height 12 cm.]\nWhat is the total area of the banner in square centimeters?\nRecord your answer and fill in the bubbles on your answer document. [Griddable — answer: 290]"'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-33","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-33.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-33","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"290"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-33)."'::jsonb),
('b77f6a52-d10e-5fd0-9a9f-31317d84b7d1'::uuid,'est2','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The first four stages of a pattern of arcs from congruent circles are shown below.\n[Figure: Stage 1: 180° arc; Stage 2: 120° arc; Stage 3: 80° arc; Stage 4: 53.3̄° arc.]\nIf this pattern continues, which expression can be used to find the degree measure of the arc in Stage n?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"20(10 − n²)"},{"key":"B","text":"180(1/3)^(n−1)"},{"key":"C","text":"60(4 − n)"},{"key":"D","text":"270(2/3)^n"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-35","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-35.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-35","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-35)."'::jsonb),
('f686a95a-82df-597c-b09b-c7a9de150930'::uuid,'est2','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"In the figure below, k ∥ t and k ⊥ q.\n[Figure: Lines k and t are parallel, both slanting up to the right; line q crosses k at C and t at A; line r passes through C and B (B on t); D is on line k above C.]\nBased on this information, which statement can be proved true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"∠ACB ≅ ∠ABC"},{"key":"G","text":"△CAB is an acute triangle."},{"key":"H","text":"∠DCB ≅ ∠BCA"},{"key":"J","text":"△CAB is a right triangle."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-36","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-36.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-36","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-36)."'::jsonb),
('21b95c5a-18f4-5a88-bd6f-43e9eea64d81'::uuid,'est2','"Solid Geometry & Volume"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The main entrance to the Louvre art museum is shaped like a pyramid. The pyramid is 71 feet tall and has a slant height of approximately 91 feet. Each side of the square base measures 115 feet.\n[Figure: Square pyramid with height shown perpendicular to the base.]\nWhich of the following is closest to the lateral surface area of the pyramid?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"20,930 ft²"},{"key":"B","text":"16,330 ft²"},{"key":"C","text":"10,465 ft²"},{"key":"D","text":"34,155 ft²"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-37","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Solid Geometry & Volume","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-37.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-37","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-37)."'::jsonb),
('86d05ead-c837-5b82-9deb-666d0c7b8b6f'::uuid,'est2','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The diagram below shows the arcs and segments used to construct △ABC, given line k.\n[Figure: Line k with points A, W, B on it; arcs centered at A and B with the same radius intersect at C above the line; segment CW drawn perpendicular to AB; arcs suggest CA = CB.]\nBased on this construction, which statement is not true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"∠AWC is complementary to ∠CWB."},{"key":"B","text":"△CWB is a right triangle."},{"key":"C","text":"△ACB is isosceles."},{"key":"D","text":"m∠CAB = m∠CBA"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-39","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-39.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-39","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-39)."'::jsonb),
('bdc4b79e-262b-56c8-952d-93a714d39949'::uuid,'est2','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Parallelogram ABCD was transformed to form parallelogram A′B′C′D′.\n[Figure: ABCD with A(−7, 1), B(−7, 6), C(−4, 8), D(−4, 3); A′B′C′D′ with A′(7, −2), B′(7, 3), C′(4, 5), D′(4, 0).]\nWhich rule describes the transformation that was used to form parallelogram A′B′C′D′?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"(x, −y)"},{"key":"G","text":"(−x, y)"},{"key":"H","text":"(x + 6, −y)"},{"key":"J","text":"(−x, y − 3)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-40","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-40.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-40","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-40)."'::jsonb),
('4de80077-c559-52bd-8467-ff7e61d39eda'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Within a square section of land, a landscaper will build a path, as represented by the shaded section in the diagram below.\n[Figure: Square; from the top-left corner a 15 ft segment runs to the right side at 30° below the top edge; from a point on the left side a parallel 15 ft segment runs to the bottom-right corner at 30° above the bottom edge; the shaded band lies between them; 60° angles marked where the segments meet the sides; y = the unshaded portion of the right side.]\nWhich measure is closest to the value of y?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"5.5 ft"},{"key":"B","text":"3.1 ft"},{"key":"C","text":"4.3 ft"},{"key":"D","text":"7.5 ft"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-41","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-41.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-41","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-41)."'::jsonb),
('aa8f23f9-ebf0-54ee-acde-9e18484d901d'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The top of a bench has a length of 5 ft and a width of 2 ft. A second bench is similar to the first bench. The top of the second bench is 3 ft wide. What is the length of the top of the second bench?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"6 ft"},{"key":"G","text":"7 ft"},{"key":"H","text":"4.5 ft"},{"key":"J","text":"7.5 ft"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-42","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-42)."'::jsonb),
('b1398cb8-ec59-5160-b631-6c1c322464ba'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','numeric','"△ABC and △PQR are shown in the diagram below.\n[Figure: △ABC with m∠A = 50°, m∠C = 60°; AB has double tick marks, AC has triple tick marks, BC has a single tick mark. △PQR: RP has triple tick marks, RQ has a single tick mark, PQ has double tick marks.]\nBased on the information provided in the diagram, what is m∠P in degrees?\n[Griddable — answer: 50]"'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-43","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-43.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-43","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"50"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-43)."'::jsonb),
('5ff25612-c1fd-5d8d-b92c-17e35152c533'::uuid,'est2','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A civil engineer is drawing a plan for the location and length of a new underground sewer pipe on a coordinate grid. The pipe on the plan will run from point N (a, −2) to point P (1, b) on the coordinate grid. Which expression represents the shortest distance between N and P in units?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"(a + 2)² + (1 − b)²"},{"key":"G","text":"(1 − a)² + (b + 2)²"},{"key":"H","text":"√((a + 2)² + (1 − b)²)"},{"key":"J","text":"√((1 − a)² + (b + 2)²)"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-44","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-44)."'::jsonb),
('47a0f77a-f5a6-53a2-9a65-3be1fbc76879'::uuid,'est2','"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"In the spinner modeled below, Sector 1 has twice the area of Sector 3.\n[Figure: Circle divided into 4 sectors labeled 1, 2, 3, 4; Sector 2 is a quarter (right-angle mark); a right-angle mark also appears at the boundary by Sector 4, making Sector 4 a quarter; spinner arrow points into Sector 4.]\nIf the arrow is spun once, what is the probability that the arrow will land in Sector 1?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"1/3"},{"key":"B","text":"1/4"},{"key":"C","text":"1/6"},{"key":"D","text":"2/3"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-45","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Statistics, Probability & Data Analysis","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-45.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-45","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-45)."'::jsonb),
('eb50a522-3a7d-51f8-8708-16c22f3e9bcf'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'easy','mcq','"A triangle is enlarged by multiplying each of its dimensions by 4. Based on this information, which of the following statements is true?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"The perimeter of the new triangle is 12 times the perimeter of the original triangle."},{"key":"G","text":"The perimeter of the new triangle is 16 times the perimeter of the original triangle."},{"key":"H","text":"The perimeter of the new triangle is 18 times the perimeter of the original triangle."},{"key":"J","text":"The perimeter of the new triangle is 4 times the perimeter of the original triangle."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-46","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"J"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-46)."'::jsonb),
('bc6ddfad-0cad-5bdd-93fb-dcf0e47eba9b'::uuid,'est2','"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The slopes of the sides of quadrilateral ABCD are shown in the table below.\n[Table: Side AB slope 2/5; BC slope −2/5; CD slope 2/5; AD slope −5/2.]\nWhich statement describes the relationships between the sides of the quadrilateral?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"AD is parallel to BC, but AB is not parallel to CD."},{"key":"B","text":"AB is parallel to CD, but AD is not parallel to BC."},{"key":"C","text":"AB is parallel to CD, and AD is parallel to BC."},{"key":"D","text":"AD is not parallel to BC, and AB is not parallel to CD."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-47","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Coordinate Geometry, Circles & Conics","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-47.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-47","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"B"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-47)."'::jsonb),
('3796b34f-f827-5910-99bd-3a811a5e9fba'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Two motorcycles start at the same point. One motorcycle travels 15 km due north and stops. The second motorcycle travels 32 km due west and stops. Which value is closest to the distance between the motorcycles when they stop?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"28.3 km"},{"key":"G","text":"47.0 km"},{"key":"H","text":"35.3 km"},{"key":"J","text":"21.9 km"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-48","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"H"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-48)."'::jsonb),
('d9c95692-03f0-59a8-b8cb-4182f5e176d1'::uuid,'est2','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"A town wants to fence in a rectangular section of a park. The table shows five possible plans for the dimensions of this fenced section. The changes in the width and the length of these plans follow a pattern.\n[Table: Plan 1: W 16, L 34, A 544; Plan 2: W 18, L 32, A 576; Plan 3: W 20, L 30, A 600; Plan 4: W 22, L 28, A 616; Plan 5: W 24, L 26, A 624.]\nIf six additional plans are added to the table and follow the same pattern, which conclusion is not correct?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"The area of one of the additional plans exceeds 624 square feet."},{"key":"B","text":"The area of one of the additional plans is less than 544 square feet."},{"key":"C","text":"The area in Plan 6 is the same as the area in Plan 5."},{"key":"D","text":"The area in Plan 7 is less than the area in Plan 6."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-49","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-49.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-49","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"A"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-49)."'::jsonb),
('eebeb459-0402-5a4d-9721-7b5d44b975f3'::uuid,'est2','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"Points A, B, C, and D are the vertices of a square. Points E and F are the centers of two congruent semicircles that are tangent to each other at point G.\n[Figure: On a coordinate grid, square with A(−4, 4), B(−4, −2), C(2, −2), D(2, 4); E(−4, 1) and F(2, 1) are centers of two semicircles of radius 3 tangent at G(−1, 1); the shaded regions are the parts of the square outside the semicircles.]\nWhich value is closest to the area of the shaded regions?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"F","text":"7.7 units²"},{"key":"G","text":"4.3 units²"},{"key":"H","text":"17.2 units²"},{"key":"J","text":"64.3 units²"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-50","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-50.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-50","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"F"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-50)."'::jsonb),
('1ec70a23-8610-55ca-bb30-d3038b273ab5'::uuid,'est2','"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations"'::jsonb,'medium','mcq','"The diagram below represents one layout of a hexagonal swimming pool that contains a drain at point D in the center of a rectangular yard.\n[Figure: A horizontal hexagon inside a vertical rectangle, with point D at its center.]\nOther layouts are also being considered. Which layout is the result of a 90° counterclockwise rotation of the original layout using D as the center of rotation?\n[Figure: Four answer choices A–D, each a rectangle containing a hexagon in a different orientation; choice D shows the hexagon rotated 90° counterclockwise (vertical orientation with the same clockwise vertex order).]"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Layout A"},{"key":"B","text":"Layout B"},{"key":"C","text":"Layout C"},{"key":"D","text":"Layout D"}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"moh_geo.md","source_sha256":"fd550cf9acb0c34c08b3bebd88f0b67714179fd7b1d4644f694d4388df535983","source_question_id":"MG-51","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Plane Geometry, Measurement & Transformations","image":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xxx64220-debug/ghoneem-math-portal/main/assets/question-bank/MG-51.png","image_alt":"Original graph, diagram, or table for MG-51","visual_verified":true}'::jsonb,'"D"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from moh_geo.md (MG-51)."'::jsonb),
